    estimatedMinutes: 4,
    approachTags: ["evidence"],
    sensitive: false,
    bodyMarkdown: "Basis: evidence — moral-incongruence research\n\nMost people arrive with a borrowed reason. I should stop. It's bad. Good people don't do this. The word \"should\" is the problem. A should is a reason you got from someone else: a parent, a religion, an article, a partner's face. It sits heavy on you, and it vanishes the moment you are tired or alone at 1 a.m. It has no grip when you need it.\n\n## Shame speeds it up, it does not stop it\n\nMost people assume that feeling bad enough will make them stop. The research says the opposite. The best-repeated finding in this whole field is called moral incongruence: a lot of the pain people feel comes not from how much they use, but from the gap between what they do and what they believe they should do. People who treat it as a moral failure report more shame, call themselves \"addicts\" more, and relapse more. Joshua Grubbs and his colleagues have shown this across many studies (Grubbs et al., 2019).\n\nHere is the loop. The behaviour makes shame. Shame hurts. And porn is one of the things you reach for to numb pain. So the guilt you pile on as a \"deterrent\" becomes fuel for the next time. You feel disgusting, feeling disgusting is unbearable, and the fastest way you know to stop feeling it is the thing you feel disgusting about. Round it goes. Shame is not a brake. It is a trapdoor.\n\nThis is not special to porn. Across addictions, people high in shame tend to do worse, while self-forgiveness tracks with recovery. The drill-sergeant model of change is popular and wrong, from drinking to overeating to this.\n\n## \"If I drop the moral reason, am I letting myself off?\"\n\nNo. Dropping shame is not dropping seriousness. You can want this badly and hold yourself to it without self-contempt. You are setting down the verdict on your character, not the commitment to change.\n\nYou are also trading a weak reason for a strong one. Watch how each behaves when you slip. \"I'm a bad person\" collapses at the first slip, because the slip seems to prove it, and once you are already condemned, why stop? \"I want my mornings back\" survives the same slip, because a slip does not disprove it. One reason shatters on failure. The other bends and stays standing. You want the reason that survives your worst night.\n\n## \"Does morality not matter, then?\"\n\nIt can matter. The point is narrow: using your values as a whip against yourself is the part that backfires. Many faiths already know this, which is why their healthier forms lead with grace, not condemnation. Keep your values. Stop using them as a weapon, because the weapon feeds the thing you want gone.\n\nSo your reason is allowed to be ordinary. I'm tired of the fog. I want to feel something real with a person. I don't like how much time this quietly eats. Those point at a life you want, not a court you are standing in. A life you want is something you can still feel at 1 a.m., long after a verdict has evaporated.\n\n## Who this is for\n\nThis matters most if your distress is heavily moral or religious, because that is where the shame loop is best documented and does the most harm. The move is not to drop your faith, but to stop swinging it at yourself. If your reasons are already practical, this just confirms you are on solid ground. Either way, today's job is the same: find the reason that survives your worst evening, and make it concrete enough to ache a little. The ache is the grip.\n\n## The action\n\nWrite one honest, non-moral reason in the form \"I want this because ___.\" Save it. The app will show it back to you on hard nights.\n\n## Sources & basis\n\nMoral incongruence as a driver of distress and self-perceived \"addiction\": Grubbs et al. (2019), in the evidence base. Cross-addiction parallel: shame-proneness tracks worse addiction outcomes while self-forgiveness tracks recovery (general finding), stated qualitatively.",
    reflectionPrompt: "Finish this in your own words, concretely enough to feel on a bad night: \"I want this because ______.\" Keep it short enough to read in ten seconds. We'll bring it back when the pull is strongest.",
    reflectionFields: [{ key: 'reflection', label: 'In your own words', type: 'longText' }],
