    bodyMarkdown: "Basis: evidence — ACT values\n\n\"Stop watching porn\" and \"be someone who is present with the people I love\" can describe the same change. Under pressure they behave completely differently. The first is an away-from goal. The second is a toward goal. Which one you carry decides a lot.\n\n## You can't run from an empty room\n\nAway-from goals spring a trap at the worst moment: the moment you succeed. You get through the evening without reaching for it, and now you are standing in an empty room. You removed the behaviour, but you also removed what it was doing for you: the wind-down, the escape, the way you got yourself to sleep. Your nervous system hates that empty space and grabs the fastest thing to fill it, which is, reliably, the exact behaviour you just dropped. Success itself makes the emptiness that undoes it.\n\nA toward goal fixes this by giving the freed-up time somewhere to go. If the goal is \"be present with my kids at night,\" the room is not empty; your kids are in it. The energy has a destination, so it stops pooling and looking for the nearest drain.\n\n## The evidence, and why fighting backfires\n\nThis is the core of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy, or ACT, which has the strongest controlled evidence of anything tested for problematic porn use, and which works the same way across other compulsive behaviours. The instruction is to move toward what you value, not to fight down what you do not want. The reason matters: suppression rebounds.\n\nTry it now. For ten seconds, do not think about a white bear. You can't. Watching for the forbidden thought is thinking about it. Daniel Wegner showed this in 1987: people told to suppress a thought had it bounce back harder (Wegner et al., 1987). Holding an urge down works the same way. It takes constant effort, and the second the effort drops, when you are tired, drunk, or low, it rebounds louder than before. Pushing against a craving keeps it bolted to the center of the frame. Walking toward something you want moves it to the edge, where it shrinks from neglect.\n\n## \"But I really do just need to stop\"\n\nTrue, the behaviour has to change. The point is not to swap stopping for vague hope and wait for the porn to drift away. It is that \"stop,\" held alone, is unstable, and a destination is what steadies it.\n\nTwo people cross the same icy car park. One stares at the ice, braced and rigid, and slips, because he is fixed on the thing he fears. The other keeps his eyes on the warm doorway ahead and crosses fine. Same ice, opposite results, decided by where the eyes point. Notice the second man still avoids the ice. He just does it by aiming at the door. You are not dropping the \"stop.\" You reach it sideways, by chasing the thing that makes stopping worth it.\n\n## Who this is for\n\nAlmost everyone, but especially if you have tried and failed at pure willpower before. That is the away-from model collapsing into the empty room. If \"just stop\" has never once stuck despite real effort, this is likely why, and the fix is a destination, not more willpower. Behind every \"stop ___\" there is a \"so that I can ___.\" That second half is the engine.\n\n## The action\n\nRewrite your goal as a toward statement. Take your \"stop ___\" and turn it into a \"become / build / be present for ___.\"\n\n## Sources & basis\n\nThe toward-values stance and the rebound problem with suppression are core to ACT, the best-supported approach for problematic pornography use (Twohig & Crosby, 2010; Crosby & Twohig, 2016). The white-bear suppression effect is Wegner et al. (1987).",
    reflectionPrompt: "Write the second half of your own sentence: \"I want to stop ______ so that I can ______.\" The part after \"so that\" is the one to keep where you can see it.",
    reflectionFields: [{ key: 'reflection', label: 'In your own words', type: 'longText' }],
