    sensitive: false,
    bodyMarkdown: "Basis: evidence — hyperbolic discounting\n\nThe urge rarely peaks when life is good. It peaks after a bad day: a rejection, a deadline gone wrong, a fight, a long grey stretch of nothing. If you can go two weeks feeling strong and then get flattened on one rough Tuesday, this is why. And it is not weakness. It is closer to arithmetic.\n\n## Two systems, and a bad day tilts both\n\nYour brain runs a fast system that wants relief now and a slow system that plans for the future. The fast system's bias has a name: present bias. We overvalue a reward we can get this second and steeply discount a bigger one later. Offered a small treat now or a bigger one next month, a lot of us grab the small one, knowing it is the worse deal.\n\nOn a good day, the slow system wins. The future feels real, so a small hit is easy to wave off. A bad day tilts both systems at once. First, the future goes blurry, because pain shrinks the horizon and \"nothing matters anyway\" starts to sound true. Second, the craving for relief spikes, because you are in real discomfort and want out now. So the future's pull weakens exactly when the present's pull jumps, and porn is right there as the fastest relief you know. Of course the pull is strongest then.\n\n## Stress is the universal relapse trigger\n\nThis is not a porn quirk. It is one of the most solid findings across every addiction studied. In the drug and alcohol research, stress is repeatedly named as a top driver of relapse (Sinha, 2007). Brain scans give a physical reason: the areas that handle stress and the areas that handle craving overlap, so a stressed brain is, almost literally, more craving-prone. The same shows up in alcohol, nicotine, heroin, gambling, and food, because the machinery is shared. When a bad day knocks you sideways and the urge shows up on cue, that is the most predictable thing in the whole field, not a private flaw.\n\n## \"So am I doomed on bad days?\"\n\nNo, and this is where knowing the mechanism pays off. Predictable is the opposite of doomed. If the spike reliably follows the bad day, the worst time to fight it is in the moment, exhausted and in pain, when willpower is weakest. That is like choosing to have the hardest argument of your life while drunk. The winnable version happens earlier, while you are calm: you see the storm coming and decide in advance what you will do.\n\nThink of a sailor and a forecast. You cannot stop the storm. But on the calm morning you can reef the sails and lash down the deck, so you are not improvising in the gale. Your bad days are the forecast. Decide now that bad days are when the phone sleeps in the kitchen, when you walk before any other choice, when you call a friend instead of sitting alone. A decision made calm at noon beats ten tries at willpower at midnight.\n\n## Who this is for\n\nThis is for the common pattern where slips cluster around stress and low mood, not desire. If your log shows the urges arriving on your worst days, this lesson is your map, and much of the programme is really about pre-deciding for those days. If your urges come more from boredom or plain habit, weight the environment lessons instead. Today's job is small: see the pattern clearly enough to stop reading a predictable spike as proof of a broken character.\n\n## The action\n\nFor one week, before each urge, log your mood (1–5) and what happened that day. You're building a map of your own spikes.\n\n## Sources & basis\n\nPresent bias / hyperbolic discounting is established behavioural economics. Cross-addiction parallel: stress is a major, well-replicated relapse trigger with overlapping stress and craving circuitry (Sinha, 2007). Negative mood as an antecedent also appears in the evidence base (Part B).",
    reflectionPrompt: "Name your three most reliable \"bad-day\" triggers, the specific situations that flatten you. Write them down. Knowing the shape of your own bad day is half of seeing the spike coming.",
    reflectionFields: [{ key: 'reflection', label: 'In your own words', type: 'longText' }],
