    sensitive: false,
    bodyMarkdown: "Basis: plausible — future-self continuity\n\nWe treat our own future self almost like a different person, a vague acquaintance we do not owe much. Future-you deals with the consequences and pays the bill. Present-you just wants tonight to be a little easier. It is like running up a tab in someone else's name: the relief is yours now, and the cost lands on a stranger who happens to share your face.\n\n## The craving lives in that gap\n\nThat split quietly drives a lot of late-night decisions. When future-you feels like a stranger, handing him the wasted morning and the fog costs almost nothing, because he is not quite you. So the trade the craving offers, big relief now in exchange for a bill later, looks like a bargain, precisely because the person who pays does not feel real yet.\n\nNaming it shows the counter-move. The craving's whole pitch is some version of \"it's free, someone else covers it, that's a problem for later.\" Make future-you real enough to genuinely care about, and the pitch falls apart, because you do not hand a bill to someone you love. You protect them.\n\n## An honest, modest finding\n\nThe research here is real but modest, which is exactly why this lesson is tagged plausible rather than evidence. Hal Hershfield and colleagues study \"future-self continuity.\" People who feel more connected to their future selves tend to make more patient choices: they save more money and look after their health better. Making the future self vivid, by writing to it or even viewing a digitally aged photo of your own face, can nudge people toward longer-term decisions. So this is a studied effect, not folklore. But it is a gentle lever, not a proven cure, so keep it as a quiet nudge rather than a heavy hitter.\n\n## \"Isn't a letter to myself just journaling fluff?\"\n\nIt can feel that way, and if it does nothing for you, drop it. But notice what it mechanically does. It takes \"the future,\" an abstraction your brain barely registers, and turns it into a specific person with a face, a morning, and a stake in tonight. That aims straight at the gap the craving was using. You are not writing for catharsis. You are making future-you real, so that next time the craving offers its free-now-pay-later deal, the \"later\" has a face attached, and stiffing a face is much harder than stiffing an abstraction.\n\n## \"What if I don't like my future self?\"\n\nIn a low patch, imagining a year out can bring dread rather than warmth. If that is you, write the letter the other way, as a plea rather than a thank-you: what does next-year-you most need today-you to protect or start, so that he is not still stuck in the same place a year from now? Either direction works, because both make the future person real.\n\n## Who this is for\n\nThis suits people moved more by connection and imagination than by columns of numbers, and people for whom the abstract future simply does not feel real, which is most of us. If cold cost-counting (tomorrow's lesson) leaves you unmoved, this warmer route to the same place may land harder. Write a short letter from one-year-out-you back to today-you, name what this year freed up, and keep it for the hard nights.\n\n## The action\n\nWrite a short letter from one-year-from-now-you back to today-you. What did this year free up? Save it; re-read on hard nights.\n\n## Sources & basis\n\nFuture-self continuity is a studied effect: greater felt connection to the future self is associated with more patient, long-term choices, and vividness interventions can shift them (Hershfield and colleagues), used here at the \"plausible\" level. Specific neuroimaging claims are deliberately not asserted.",
    reflectionPrompt: "In one line, name what future-you (a year out) most wants you to protect this year. Write it as if he's asking you directly.",
    reflectionFields: [{ key: 'reflection', label: 'In your own words', type: 'longText' }],
