    sensitive: false,
    bodyMarkdown: "Basis: plausible — commitment\n\nA reason kept in your own head is the easiest thing in the world to quietly drop. No one saw you make it, so no one sees you abandon it. It can dissolve at 1 a.m. and leave no mark. Privacy is not safety here. Privacy is the condition under which a resolution disappears without cost.\n\n## A witness changes the maths\n\nSay the reason out loud to one person and something shifts. You have turned a private wish into a small commitment with a witness, and most of us do not want to be the kind of person who quietly takes back their word. It is a modest piece of social physics, but it is real.\n\nThis is the engine under one of the most-credited tools in all of recovery: accountability. Sponsors in twelve-step programmes, quit buddies in smoking cessation, accountability partners in porn recovery, all run on the same thing. In our own evidence base, having someone to answer to is among the most-credited strategies members report helping (Part B). The common ingredient is that it drags the struggle out of the sealed dark, where shame grows, and into the open, where it shrinks.\n\n## The evidence, and its one sharp condition\n\nBe precise, because the evidence carries a caveat you cannot skip. Accountability helps when it is non-shaming. A witness who turns your slip into a humiliation manufactures the exact shame that drives the next slip. The smoking and drug research points the same way: warm, non-judgemental support aids quitting, while nagging, criticism, and policing tend to backfire and can even increase use. So this is not \"confess to someone harsh and let them punish you into shape.\" It is \"tell one safe person, once, and agree up front that slips get curiosity, not contempt.\"\n\n## \"But I can't tell anyone\"\n\nThis is the most common block, and the fear is real. Two things. First, the bar is far lower than you are imagining. This is not a confession tour, not graphic detail, not telling everyone in your life. It is one sentence, to one trusted person, once: \"I'm working on changing my relationship with porn, because I want my time and attention back.\" You can say exactly that and nothing more, and it counts.\n\nSecond, the fear that you will be met with disgust is itself made by shame, and it is almost always wrong. The usual experience is that the secret loses much of its power the instant it is spoken, and the relationship deepens rather than breaks, because being trusted with something real is how closeness grows. And if there is genuinely no safe person in your life right now, that itself is information: a non-shaming recovery community or a therapist can be the witness, which is partly what Week 6 is for.\n\n## Who this is for\n\nThis is for anyone whose pattern lives in secrecy, which is most people, and especially those who already sense the hiding is part of the engine. If saying it to a real person is genuinely impossible today, do not force it. Treat that impossibility as a measure of how isolated the behaviour has made you, and let the later social lessons build the kind of relationship that can eventually hold this one sentence.\n\n## The action\n\nTell one trusted person your reason this week. One sentence is enough.\n\n## Sources & basis\n\nNon-shaming accountability is among the most-credited strategies in the evidence base (Part B4), with the documented condition that it must be non-shaming (Part B3). Cross-addiction parallel: supportive, non-judgemental support aids smoking and substance cessation, while criticism undermines it (general finding).",
