    category: "motivation",
    estimatedMinutes: 4,
    approachTags: ["reframe"],
    sensitive: false,
    bodyMarkdown: "Basis: reframe\n\n\"It's bad for me.\" Say it out loud and notice how little happens inside you. It is probably true, and it moves you not at all, because a vague cost has no teeth. The mind cannot act on fog. It can act on a number, a scene, a specific thing it can actually see. So today we drag the costs out of the fog.\n\n## Vague costs are an anaesthetic\n\nWe keep the costs blurry on purpose, and it is not laziness, it is self-protection. \"It wastes some time\" is bearable precisely because it is fuzzy, and the fuzz lets you keep the behaviour and dodge the bill at once. So the move is to refuse the blur, not to punish yourself, but because you cannot make a clear decision about a cost you have kept out of focus.\n\nWatch what happens when you sharpen it. \"It wastes time\" is easy to shrug off. \"About forty minutes a day, call it five hours a week, most of a working day, every week, all year\" is a different sentence, because now there is a quantity, and quantities are hard to wave away. (That number is an example; find your real one.) Or take the relationship cost. \"It affects my relationship\" floats past harmlessly. \"There have been nights I turned away from being close with my partner because I would rather be alone with a screen\" lands, because it is a scene you recognise. The discomfort you feel reading a precise cost is not the enemy. It is the information.\n\n## This is a real clinical technique\n\nThis is not an app gimmick. It is a close cousin of \"decisional balance,\" a core exercise in motivational interviewing, one of the better-supported counselling styles for addictive behaviour. There, the counsellor does not lecture about the costs; they help the person lay out, in their own words, the concrete costs and benefits. The active ingredient is that the person says the specifics themselves, because self-generated reasons shift behaviour in a way someone else's warnings never do. Being told \"this is bad for you\" changes little. Hearing yourself say \"this is costing me five hours a week and the closeness I actually want\" changes something.\n\n## \"Isn't this just shame in disguise?\"\n\nA fair worry after Day 1, and the line is worth drawing sharply. Shame attacks the self: I am disgusting for doing this. The ledger attacks the price tag: this costs me five hours a week and a closeness I want. One produces self-contempt, which feeds the cycle. The other produces clarity, which weakens it. Aim for the cool, slightly bored tone of an accountant totting up a subscription you forgot you were paying, not the hot voice of a prosecutor reading charges. A quick test: if you finish feeling worthless, you have drifted into whip, so come back to plain arithmetic. If you finish clear and a little motivated, you did it right.\n\n## Who this is for\n\nThis suits people who are unmoved by warm or moral appeals but who respond to hard facts, the \"just show me the numbers\" type. It is also valuable for anyone whose use has gone so automatic that its real costs have become invisible, the way you stop seeing a mess you walk past every day. A craving always advertises its benefit and never once quotes its price. A written, specific cost ledger is the part of you that actually read the contract, kept ready to reread when the salesman comes back glowing on a hard night.\n\n## The action\n\nList the real, concrete costs, and put an actual number on the time. Keep it factual, not self-punishing.\n\n## Sources & basis\n\nReframe lesson; the \"forty minutes a day\" figure is explicitly illustrative, not a statistic. The approach mirrors \"decisional balance\" within motivational interviewing, an evidence-supported counselling style for addictive behaviour. The caution against shame rests on the moral-incongruence findings (Grubbs et al., 2019).",
    reflectionPrompt: "Write the single most uncomfortable line on your own ledger, the specific cost you'd least like to look at. That's usually the one with the most power in it.",
    reflectionFields: [{ key: 'reflection', label: 'In your own words', type: 'longText' }],
