    category: "meta",
    estimatedMinutes: 3,
    approachTags: ["evidence"],
    sensitive: false,
    bodyMarkdown: "Basis: evidence — relapse prevention\n\nHere's the uncomfortable truth this whole programme has circled: in the flooded moment, you won't be able to assemble a strategy from scratch. Your thinking is at its worst exactly when you need it most. So you don't try to think clearly under fire, you follow a plan you wrote earlier, when you could. This lesson is the capstone, where the scattered tools become one sheet you can use on your worst night.\n\n## The logic: pre-decide, consolidate, externalise\n\nNearly every idea in this programme is most useful pre-decided and written down, because a flooded brain can't generate good plans but can follow a simple one. A written plan pre-decides your responses so you're not improvising at your weakest. It consolidates the tools scattered across these lessons into one place. And it externalises the plan onto paper, so it exists outside your head, where panic can't delete it.\n\n## What goes in the plan\n\nKeep it short enough to actually use under stress. Your high-risk situations, the specific times and settings where you slip (Days 22, 41). Your early-warning signs, the apparently irrelevant decisions that are your first dominoes (Day 100). Your in-the-moment responses, written as if-then rules, plus your HALT responses and your never fail twice recovery move (Days 47, 96). Your people, who you'll message or call, including any crisis or professional contacts you saved while calm (Days 6, 83). And your why, in your own words, at the top, because it's what the plan serves.\n\n## The evidence\n\nThis isn't an app invention. It's the practical core of Marlatt and Gordon's relapse-prevention model, the most established framework in the field, built on identifying high-risk situations in advance and rehearsing specific coping responses to them. Coping responses planned and rehearsed before the high-risk moment are far more likely to be used in it than anything you try to summon on the spot. You don't improvise the fire exit in the smoke, you walk the route in advance so your feet know it.\n\n## Who this is for\n\nThis is for everyone, the place the programme's tools come together, and especially for anyone who \"goes blank\" in the moment. Spend twenty calm minutes building your plan, keep it to a page, and put it where you'll actually see it at the dangerous hour.\n\n## The action\n\nWrite a one-page relapse-prevention plan: your top high-risk situations, your if-then responses, your HALT and recovery moves, your people to contact, and your why at the top. Keep it where you'll see it.\n\n## Sources & basis\n\nRelapse-prevention planning, identifying high-risk situations in advance and rehearsing specific coping responses, is the practical core of Marlatt and Gordon's relapse-prevention model (external, well-established). It consolidates tools whose own bases are noted in their own lessons (if-then plans, HALT, stimulus control, accountability, crisis contacts). Pre-rehearsed coping is more likely to be used than improvised coping.",
    reflectionPrompt: "Draft your plan now, then name the one place you'll keep it so that flooded-you actually meets it at the dangerous hour.",
    reflectionFields: [{ key: 'reflection', label: 'In your own words', type: 'longText' }],
